        ":question/id": "62c0b89d-1d8a-5723-8092-97486825267c",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-100459",
        ":question/problem": "If ${216}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{36})}^{6a-2}$ then $a=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $216={6}^{3}$ and $36={6}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}})}^{6a-2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2(6a-2)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}={6}^{-2(6a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3a+4) & =-2(6a-2) \\\\ 9a+12 & =-12a+4 \\\\ 21a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{8}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186233866,
        ":question/difficulty": {
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
        },
        ":question/id": "8c366f3c-26d4-5a78-b6c8-77d9d1b20627",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-3362",
        ":question/problem": "If ${27}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{81})}^{-2n+8},$ then $n=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $27={3}^{3}$ and $81={3}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{3})}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}})}^{-2n+8}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the power of quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4(-2n+8)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}={3}^{-4(-2n+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2n+2) & =-4(-2n+8) \\\\ 6n+6 & =8n-32 \\\\ 2n & =38 \\\\ n & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
