        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-100462",
        ":question/problem": "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation ${9}^{d-4}={243}^{d+3}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={3}^{2}$ and $243={3}^{5}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{2})}^{d-4}={({3}^{5})}^{d+3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(d-4)}={3}^{5(d+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(d-4) & =5(d+3) \\\\ 2d-8 & =5d+15 \\\\ -3d & =23 \\\\ d & =-\\frac{23}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
