        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-100468",
        ":question/problem": "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation ${25}^{d-7}={625}^{d+10}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $25={5}^{2}$ and $625={5}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{2})}^{d-7}={({5}^{4})}^{d+10}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2(d-7)}={5}^{4(d+10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(d-7) & =4(d+10) \\\\ 2d-14 & =4d+40 \\\\ -2d & =54 \\\\ d & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$"
